E=[
("**Version note ⚠.** Targets `@testing-library/react` 16.x (`renderHook` is part of RTL since v13.1; the separate `@testing-library/react-hooks` package is legacy and does not support React 18+/19), React 19.x, Vitest 3.x/4.x. Verify `renderHook` options (`initialProps`, `wrapper`, and the Strict Mode option) in the RTL API docs for your version.",
 "**Version note.** Executed against `@testing-library/react` 16.3.3 (`renderHook` is part of RTL since v13.1; the separate `@testing-library/react-hooks` package is legacy and does not support React 18+/19), React 19.3.1 and Vitest 3.2.7 (Vitest 4/5 are released; nothing here depends on the differences). `renderHook` options `initialProps`, `wrapper` and `reactStrictMode` (as well as `configure({ reactStrictMode })`) exist in 16.3.3, verified by running."),
("⚠ *Implementation detail:* how `result.current`","*Implementation detail:* how `result.current`"),
("RTL (⚠ verify) supports enabling Strict Mode in tests (`configure({ reactStrictMode: true })` or wrapping in `<StrictMode>`).","RTL 16.3.3 supports enabling Strict Mode in tests: `renderHook(fn, { reactStrictMode: true })`, `configure({ reactStrictMode: true })`, or wrapping in `<StrictMode>`. *Verified by running:* a hook with an effect rendered 2 times, ran the effect 2 times and its cleanup 1 time on mount under Strict Mode."),
("If you call `rerender()` without args, the previous props are reused (⚠ verify for your version).","If you call `rerender()` **without args, the hook is re-rendered with `undefined` props, not the previous ones** (verified on RTL 16.3.3: a hook that destructures `({ n })` threw \"Cannot destructure property 'n' of 'undefined'\"). Pass the props you want every time."),
("- `vi.getTimerCount()` returns the number of pending fake timers: a precise leak check, justified because \"no timer left behind\" *is* the behavior being protected. ⚠ Verify availability in your Vitest version.","- `vi.getTimerCount()` returns the number of pending fake timers: a precise leak check, justified because \"no timer left behind\" *is* the behavior being protected (verified on Vitest 3.2.7: it returned 2 after two `setTimeout`s)."),
("spying on `window.localStorage.setItem` often doesn't work because `localStorage` is a special object ⚠ (behavior varies; verify in your environment).","spying on `window.localStorage.setItem` does not work in jsdom because `localStorage` is a special exotic object (verified on jsdom 30: the direct spy recorded 0 calls, the prototype spy recorded 1)."),
("- The `StorageEvent` constructor accepts `storageArea` in jsdom ⚠ verify;","- The `StorageEvent` constructor accepts `storageArea` in jsdom (verified on jsdom 30);"),
("  // React logs the error to console.error even when it propagates; silence intentionally.","  // React 19 rethrows render errors through act without logging them (verified: 0 console.error calls);\n  // React 18 logged them. Keeping the spy is harmless and makes the test portable across versions."),
("⚠ In React 19, errors thrown during render inside `act` are rethrown by `act`/`render`; behavior around uncaught render errors changed between 18 and 19 (React 19 reports uncaught errors via `window.reportError`/`console.error` hooks outside of `act`). If `toThrow` doesn't catch in your setup, render the hook under a small error boundary and assert on the captured error instead. Verify in your versions.",
 "In React 19, errors thrown during render inside `act` are rethrown by `act`/`render`, so `expect(() => renderHook(...)).toThrow(...)` works (verified on React 19.3.1 + RTL 16.3.3). Outside `act`, React 19 reports uncaught render errors through `window.reportError` (RTL 16.2+ also exposes `onCaughtError`/`onUncaughtError`/`onRecoverableError` render options). If `toThrow` doesn't catch in your setup, render the hook under a small error boundary and assert on the captured error instead."),
("latest.current = fn; // ⚠ writing a ref during render: shown to illustrate a pattern you may meet; prefer an effect (React 19 docs discourage writing refs in render)","latest.current = fn; // writing a ref during render: shown to illustrate a pattern you may meet; prefer an effect (React docs discourage writing refs in render)"),
("⚠ `isSuccess`, `data` are TanStack Query v5 fields; verify.","`isSuccess` and `data` are TanStack Query v5 fields (verified against @tanstack/react-query 5.104.5)."),
("configure({ reactStrictMode: true });   // ⚠ verify availability/name for your RTL version","configure({ reactStrictMode: true });   // available in RTL 16.3.3 (verified)"),
("(an `useEffectEvent`-like need; ⚠ `useEffectEvent` is a React 19.2 feature whose availability and rules should be verified in current docs)","(a `useEffectEvent`-like need; `useEffectEvent` is stable since React 19.2 per the react.dev 19.2 blog and is exported by react 19.3.1, but it can only be called from the component/hook that owns the Effect and must not be listed in dependency arrays; use `eslint-plugin-react-hooks` v6+ so the linter knows that)"),
]
